A timer under a dynamic `for` keeps its props as cells of the instance.

A loop the render cannot unroll makes the override a `RuntimeInstance`: one
record per iteration, its body a set of methods rather than statements spliced
into the caller. An effect's position is read by one of those methods -- the
`__effect0_down` below tests the gate and pushes the key -- so both props have
to be reachable from the record.

`interval` and `enabled` are `#[macro.construct]`, and passComponentProps used
to read that as "no cell and no setter", on the grounds that such a prop is read
while the instance is built and never again. Read while the instance is built is
not the same as read *from the constructor*, and whether that distinction bites
is the host's answer rather than the language's. A Go record's methods take a
receiver and see its fields, so `__effect0_down` named an `interval` and an
`enabled` that no record declares: `undefined: enabled` from the compiler, on
fyne and gtk4 both. **html was unaffected** -- its factory is a function and
`__effect0_down` is nested inside it, so the bare reads resolved to the
`let interval = props.interval` in the enclosing scope and always ran correctly.
The cell is there so the two Go records can spell the prop at all; on html it is
a rename of code that worked, which is the whole of that target's diff in
`timer_row_per_instance.txtar`.

The cell is the fix and the *setter* is what stays absent. That is the half the
mark is really about: `componentAbsorbs` asks after the setter, so with none the
render destroys the row and builds it again rather than writing a new period
into a running schedule -- which is what `timer_row_per_instance.txtar` pins,
and which the `deny` below restates from this side.

deny * `(?m)^\s*if \(?enabled\)? \{` -- the gate is a cell of the instance, not a bare name
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `append\(c.__effect0_desired, interval\)` -- likewise the key
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `append\(c.__effect0_desired, interval\)` -- likewise
deny * `SetInterval|__set_interval` -- a construct prop gets the cell and no setter; the row is rebuilt
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import time "sngl:time"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

component board ui.node {
    var n = 0
    var periods list<time.duration> = [100ms, 250ms]

    for var p = periods {
        time.timer(interval=p, enabled=true, @tick {
            n += 1
        })
    }

    ui.text(value=string(n))
}

ui.window(title="loop") {
    board()
}
